<td id="S2.T1.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;">Ergodic Hyperfunctions</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;">Soft Target Matching</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">Data Reliance</th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FE0000;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;">Low (Often Data-free)</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;">Moderate (Calibration)</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3166FF;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;">High (Fine-tuning)</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FE0000;--ltx-bg-color:#DAE8FC;">Low (Direct Encoding)</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.2.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3166FF;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFC7;">Very High (Teacher Output)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Low-rank Decomposition</span>: Recent studies in low-rank decomposition have focused on automating the rank-selection process, which was traditionally a manual trial-and-error effort <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Modern frameworks utilize sensitivity analysis to identify how much redundancy exists in each part of the network by measuring the Hessian trace of a layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Recently, low-rank decomposition has been intensively used in large models. A representative work is LoRA, which freezes the pre-trained model weights and injects trainable rank decomposition matrices into each layer, reducing the number of trainable parameters for downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Moreover, TensorGPT is a training-free model compression approach based on the Tensor-Train Decomposition, which reduces the number of parameters by a factor of 2.31 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quantization</span>: The frontier of quantization research is to push towards extreme low-bit regimes, giving rise to Binarized Neural Networks (BNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and Ternary Quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. BNNs represent weights and activations using just a single bit (e.g., <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>), allowing for the replacement of expensive floating-point multiplications with bitwise XNOR and population count operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Seminal works like XNOR-Net introduced the straight-through estimator to handle the non-differentiability of the sign function during backpropagation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. While extreme quantization often incurs a performance penalty on complex tasks like ImageNet classification, hybrid strategies like quantization-aware distillation have been developed, achieving high accuracy recovery even at 4-bit precision for massive models like Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pruning</span>: The pruning workflow typically involves an iterative process: a pre-trained model is evaluated to identify unimportant parameters to prune. Then, the model is fine-tuned to allow the remaining weights to compensate for the loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Recent innovations have introduced task-agnostic pruning methods and gradient-based importance measures, such as SparseGPT, which allow for the pruning of models with billions of parameters in a few hours without extensive retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Global pruning techniques further enhance efficiency by applying a single threshold across the entire network, allowing the pruning rate to vary between layers based on their relative information density <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ergodic Dynamic Systems</span>: Ergodic dynamic systems represent a novel approach in model compression research that redefines the problem as the issue of parsimonious parameter representation via a hyperfunction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. By identifying a suitable dynamic system, the weights of a large network can be encoded as a set of hyperparameters <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and a hyperfunction <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, where each parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered via <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}=h(\theta;n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}=h(\theta;n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Currently, this type of method has been successfully applied to compress a LLaMA series model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Segment Anything Models (SAMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and large images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em">∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Knowledge Distillation</span>:
KD is highly effective for compressing LLMs. Recent advancements include “online distillation” methods, which train multiple student branches simultaneously without a static pre-trained teacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>; and “dataset distillation”, which condenses massive training sets into compact synthetic datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. While KD is highly effective at preserving emergent reasoning in LLMs, it can be computationally expensive to train the teacher-student pair, and the performance of the student is inherently capped by the capacity of the teacher and the compatibility of their architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">While <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> also relies on neural networks, it differs fundamentally from KD and self-KD in two key ways. Primarily, <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> operates on a “compression-decompression” paradigm that dynamically reconstructs weights during inference, while KD only uses the student model after training. Furthermore, <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> is applied layer by layer—using a separate network to learn each layer’s weights—whereas KD optimizes a single student model to replicate the final predictions of the large model. Lastly, <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> is a data-free compression method, while most KD methods rely heavily on data except for self-KD. Data-free model compression (DFMC) attempts to compress the model without accessing to the original training data, which poses privacy risks in sensitive domains such as healthcare and finance. Prevalent DFMC strategies primarily include pruning, quantization, model merging, and ergodic dynamic systems. For instance, unifying Data-Free Compression (UDFC) executes pruning and quantization jointly without requiring data access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Similarly, methods like ZipIt! <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> and Model Folding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> reduce model size by merging redundant computational units within the original networks. Data-Free Knowledge Distillation (DFKD) takes a different approach, employing synthetic data generated via GANs or optimized noise to facilitate student model fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Lastly, previously mentioned Hyper-Compression is also the DFMC method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, despite all model compression methods share the fundamental goal of reducing model size and computational cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, they differ in mechanisms. A comparative overview of their key properties is provided in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ II-A Model Compression Methods ‣ II Related Works ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>. Building on this landscape, this paper introduces a unifying mathematical framework to provide a systematic and in-depth understanding of these diverse compression techniques, which is novel in the field of model compression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Neural Representation and Hypernetworks</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) parameterize a signal as a continuous function that maps input coordinates to output signal values (e.g., RGB pixel intensities for images). To enhance the expressivity of INRs, recent studies have proposed specialized activation functions and architectural modules. For instance, sinusoidal (SIREN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and wavelet (WIRE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> activation functions were designed to capture high-frequency details. Tancik <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> introduced a positional encoding scheme that projects inputs into Fourier space, enabling the network to learn high-frequency functions within low-dimensional domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, Mehta <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> proposed modulated periodic activations to strengthen local feature representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. Beyond architectural improvements, the flexibility of INRs allows for seamless integration with downstream compression techniques to further enhance compression ratios. These include quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and Bayesian coding approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The hypernetwork (often shortened to hypernet, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>) is a small meta-network designed to generate the weights for a larger, primary target network. Instead of learning its parameters directly via gradient descent, the target network has its weights dynamically produced by the hypernetwork, which takes an input (such as an embedding or task ID) and outputs the weight matrices for the main model. This architecture allows for dynamic and adaptable model behavior, enables weight sharing across layers for efficiency, and is particularly useful in meta-learning scenarios where a model must quickly adapt to new tasks by having its weights generated on the fly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Examples include one-shot generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, component-wise generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, chunk-wise generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, and multiple generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. However, to the best of our knowledge, the idea of hypernet was not used for model compression before <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. On the one hand, the proposed <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2small</span> diverges fundamentally from the conventional paradigm of INRs in two key aspects. First, whereas INRs employ a neural network to directly represent data, <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2small</span> utilizes a network to generate the parameters of another target network. In this sense, <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2small</span> can be viewed as a generalization of the INR concept, extending its application from data to network. Second, two approaches operate in opposite scaling regimes relative to their targets. INRs often employ networks larger than the data itself to achieve higher fidelity, while <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2small</span> is explicitly designed for compression, producing a compact representation of a larger target network. On the other hand, the proposed <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2small</span> is also different from the idea of hypernet. The former is for static compression of the target network, while the latter is for the dynamic tuning of the target network.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose a unifying mathematical definition of model compression as a neural function approximation problem within a measure-theoretic framework. Our overarching insight is to interpret model compression as the projection from a large collection of parameters to a small collection of parameters. Under this umbrella, we present two primary theoretical results: i) <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">universal compressibility theorem</span> proving that these compression methods (low-rank, pruning, quantization, ergodic dynamic systems, knowledge distillation) can rigorously restore the original weights by the inverse projection with an error <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, which warrants compressibility; and ii) <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">structural equivalence theorem</span> demonstrating that all five techniques are mathematically equivalent to specific architecture of neural networks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preliminaries</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by establishing the geometric foundations of our framework, distinguishing between the original parameter space and the bounded parameter sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Parameter Space and Sets)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\cong\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\cong\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space equipped with the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the unconstrained parameter space. i) We define the original parameter set <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> as a compact subset of weights, satisfying <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\theta^{*}\|_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\theta^{*}\|_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. ii) The compressed weight is defined as <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\theta}^{\dagger}\in\Sigma^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\theta}^{\dagger}\in\Sigma^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. iii) The reconstructed weight of the compressed model is defined as <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}\in\hat{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}\in\hat{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard Lebesgue measure on <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, restricted to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Distance Function)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define a distance function as <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\theta^{*},{\theta}^{\dagger})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\theta^{*},{\theta}^{\dagger})</annotation></semantics></math>, we utilize the Euclidean distance to quantify the approximation error between the target weight <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the compressed weight <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\theta}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\theta}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case of low-rank decomposition, quantization, pruning, and ergodic systems, the distance function is</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger})\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger})\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> recovers the target weights from the compressed weight.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case of knowledge distillation, the distance function is</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)-F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)-F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are the teacher network’s and the student network’s output with respect to </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sets Size)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{Pointsize}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Pointsize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Pointsize}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the average number of bits required to encode a point in a given set. We define the size functional of a parameter set as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)=\texttt{Pointsize}(\Sigma)\times\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Pointsize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)=\texttt{Pointsize}(\Sigma)\times\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Model Compression Mapping)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the mapping function as <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. For the target optimal weights <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> compresses the target weights to</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\theta}^{\dagger}=g(\theta^{*})\in\Sigma^{\dagger}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\theta}^{\dagger}=g(\theta^{*})\in\Sigma^{\dagger},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and reconstructs the target weights</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\theta}}=g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger})\in\hat{\Sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\theta}}=g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger})\in\hat{\Sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The reconstructed weights must be bounded by a given error tolerance <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})&lt;\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})&lt;\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, we slightly abuse the notation. While <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the compression and reconstruction mappings respectively, they do not form a strict mathematical inverse pair due to the lossy nature of compression.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sets of Compression Methods)<span id="Thmdefinition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the compressed parameter sets <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from five primary compression paradigms.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Low-Rank Set (<math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The set of weight matrices with a bounded </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{rank}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{rank}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}\mid\texttt{rank}(\theta^{\dagger})\leq r\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}\mid\texttt{rank}(\theta^{\dagger})\leq r\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Quantization Set (<math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The uniform lattice defined by a bit-width </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\theta^{\dagger}_{i}\in\{-R+j\Delta\mid j\in\{0,1,\dots,2^{b}-1\}\}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\theta^{\dagger}_{i}\in\{-R+j\Delta\mid j\in\{0,1,\dots,2^{b}-1\}\}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Pruning Set (<math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The set of subspaces with at most </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> non-zero elements.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\|\theta^{\dagger}\|_{0}\leq N\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\|\theta^{\dagger}\|_{0}\leq N\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Ergodic Set (<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The set of trajectories generated by a chaotic map </span><math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}=\{k\in\mathbb{N}\mid\hat{\theta}=T^{k}(\theta_{0}^{\dagger}),\,\theta_{0}^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}=\{k\in\mathbb{N}\mid\hat{\theta}=T^{k}(\theta_{0}^{\dagger}),\,\theta_{0}^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Knowledge Distillation Set (<math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span><span id="S3.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The parameter set represents a smaller “student” architecture.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\{0\}^{D-d}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}=\{\theta^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\{0\}^{D-d}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theory I: Universal Compressibility</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish the theoretical validity of model compression. We propose a unifying theorem that shows that the compression sets (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>) defined by five primary compression methods rigorously reduce the set size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> of the original set, and approximating the original parameters by the reconstructed parameters is bounded by a given error
tolerance <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. An illustration framework of this theory is depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III-B Theory I: Universal Compressibility ‣ III A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29768v1/Theory1.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:22.765em; height:17.080em;aspect-ratio:302/227;" width="302" height="227"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>The theoretical framework of Theorem 1.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Universal Compressibility)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the original weight parameter set be <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For any original weight <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and error tolerance <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists paired mapping functions <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{-1}:\Sigma^{\dagger}\to\hat{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{-1}:\Sigma^{\dagger}\to\hat{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to Low-Rank Decomposition, Quantization, Pruning, Ergodic Dynamic Systems, or Knowledge Distillation, such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger})\quad\text{and}\quad\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})&lt;\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger})\quad\text{and}\quad\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})&lt;\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove both the set size <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger})</annotation></semantics></math> and the approximation bound <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for each model compression method.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>Low-Rank Set</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The compression mapping function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, truncating to rank <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size Reduction:</span> The set <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic variety of dimension <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(n+m-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(n+m-r)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is strictly less than <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nm</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the Lebesgue measure of the compressed set is zero: <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{r})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approximation:</span> By the Eckart-Young-Mirsky theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, the approximation error for <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{r}=g^{-1}_{r}(\theta^{\dagger}_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{r}=g^{-1}_{r}(\theta^{\dagger}_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the tail singular values <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger}_{r})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger}_{r})\|_{2}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=r+1}^{\min(n,m)}\sigma_{i}^{2}}&lt;\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger}_{r})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}(\theta^{\dagger}_{r})\|_{2}=\sqrt{\sum_{i=r+1}^{\min(n,m)}\sigma_{i}^{2}}&lt;\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As mentioned before, <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded. Hence, the tail sum converges to 0 as <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\to\min(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\to\min(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, there exists a rank <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> such that the error is bounded by <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Quantization Set</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. The mapping <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{q}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{q}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform rounding function mapping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> onto a discrete lattice with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> levels over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-R,R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-R,R]</annotation></semantics></math>. The step size is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\frac{2R}{2^{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\frac{2R}{2^{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size Reduction:</span> <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{Pointsize}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q})=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Pointsize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Pointsize}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q})=b</annotation></semantics></math>, which is strictly smaller than the standard 32-bit floating point size of <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approximation:</span> The maximum error occurs when <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is at the center of a lattice hypercube:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}_{q}(\theta^{\dagger}_{q})\|_{2}\leq\frac{\Delta}{2}\sqrt{D}=\frac{R\sqrt{D}}{2^{b}}&lt;\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g^{-1}_{q}(\theta^{\dagger}_{q})\|_{2}\leq\frac{\Delta}{2}\sqrt{D}=\frac{R\sqrt{D}}{2^{b}}&lt;\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the term <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R\sqrt{D}}{2^{b}}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R\sqrt{D}}{2^{b}}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For sufficiently large bit-width <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, the error is <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Pruning Set</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mapping <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{p}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{p}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> applies Magnitude Pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, zeroing out all but the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> largest coefficients.</p>
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size Reduction:</span> <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of finite lower-dimensional coordinate subspaces (each of dimension <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;D</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence, the <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Lebesgue measure <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfying <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approximation:</span> The squared approximation error is the energy of the discarded weights:</p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g_{p}^{-1}({\theta^{\dagger}_{p}})\|_{2}^{2}=\sum_{j=k+1}^{D}|\theta^{*}_{j}|^{2}&lt;\epsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|\theta^{*}-g_{p}^{-1}({\theta^{\dagger}_{p}})\|_{2}^{2}=\sum_{j=k+1}^{D}|\theta^{*}_{j}|^{2}&lt;\epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weights sorted by magnitude. As the sparsity parameter <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\to D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\to D</annotation></semantics></math>, the residual tail sum vanishes to 0.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>4 </span>Ergodic Set</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mapping <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{e}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{e}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> finds the optimal initial seed <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\ll D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\ll D</annotation></semantics></math>) for an ergodic dynamical system <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S3.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size Reduction:</span> The parameter degrees of freedom are reduced from <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The intrinsic dimension of <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{erg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{erg}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, implying its measure in <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approximation:</span> We rely on the Topological Transitivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> of chaotic maps. The <span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shadowing Property</span> guarantees that for open balls <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with radius <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\tau}{\sqrt{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>τ</mi><msqrt><mi>D</mi></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\tau}{\sqrt{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a seed <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> whose trajectory visits these sets sequentially <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. This means that there exits <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E16X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\theta^{*}-T^{k}(\theta^{\dagger}_{0})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\theta^{*}-T^{k}(\theta^{\dagger}_{0})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E16Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{D}({\theta^{*}_{i}-(T^{k}(\theta_{0}^{\dagger}))_{i}})^{2}}&lt;\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{D}\frac{\tau^{2}}{D}}=\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{D}({\theta^{*}_{i}-(T^{k}(\theta_{0}^{\dagger}))_{i}})^{2}}&lt;\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{D}\frac{\tau^{2}}{D}}=\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>5 </span>Knowledge Distillation Set</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mapping <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{k}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{k}:\Sigma\to\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distillation process mapping the teacher <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to a smaller student architecture defined on a subspace of dimension <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;D</annotation></semantics></math> (zero-padded).</p>
<ul id="S3.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size Reduction:</span> <math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> operates in a <math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace embedded in <math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions. Thus, <math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{m}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, reducing the size functional <math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma)&gt;\mathfrak{s}(\Sigma^{\dagger}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approximation:</span> According to the KD learning paradigm, the network uses the outputs of the student model <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> to mimic the output of the teacher model <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the input signal as <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and the loss function of two neural networks is equal to the distance function (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In item 2 ‣ Definition 2 (Distance Function). ‣ III-A Preliminaries ‣ III A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{kd}=\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)-F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dist</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{kd}=\texttt{dist}(\theta^{*},\theta^{\dagger})=\|F^{*}(\theta^{*},x)-F^{\dagger}(\theta^{\dagger},x)\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By the Universal Approximation Theorem, assuming sufficient student capacity, there exists a configuration <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> such that this functional loss is bounded by <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theory II: Structural Equivalence</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formally prove that five compression methods are mathematically equivalent to specific neural network architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Structural Equivalence)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the five compression methods low-rank, pruning, quantization, ergodic systems, and knowledge distillation, there exists a specific neural network compressor <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter set <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the output set of the network is isomorphic to the compressed set <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Sigma}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Sigma}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove this theorem case-by-case. In general, the classical compression mapping <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can be reformulated as a neural network compressor <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose output space constitutes the compressed set <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. An illustrative overview of this equivalence is presented in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ III-C Theory II: Structural Equivalence ‣ III A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29768v1/Theory2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/448;" width="476" height="448" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>The framework of Theorem 2.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>1 </span>Low-Rank Decomposition as Linear Bottleneck Networks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Low-rank decomposition approximates the weight matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the product of two smaller matrices <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}\approx AB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}\approx AB</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> with rank <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To map this to our framework, we construct a 2-layer linear bottle network <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> with latent input <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=I_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=I_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> Identity matrix) and parameter set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\{{\theta}^{\dagger}_{1},{\theta}^{\dagger}_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\{{\theta}^{\dagger}_{1},{\theta}^{\dagger}_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\theta}^{\dagger}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\theta}^{\dagger}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\theta}^{\dagger}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\theta}^{\dagger}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the matrices <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The forward pass is defined as a purely linear transformation:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)={\theta}_{2}^{\dagger}({\theta}_{1}^{\dagger}I_{m})={\theta}_{2}^{\dagger}{\theta}_{1}^{\dagger}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)={\theta}_{2}^{\dagger}({\theta}_{1}^{\dagger}I_{m})={\theta}_{2}^{\dagger}{\theta}_{1}^{\dagger}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The compression task becomes the optimization of the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi}\|\theta^{*}-\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi}\|\theta^{*}-\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">According to Baldi and Hornik <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, the global minimum strictly recovers the optimal rank-<math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> approximation of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> given by the Singular Value Decomposition (SVD).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>2 </span>Quantization as Staircase Activation Networks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical quantization approximates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}\approx s\cdot\text{round}(\theta^{*}/s)+z_{zero}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>round</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}\approx s\cdot\text{round}(\theta^{*}/s)+z_{zero}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> being scale factor.
We define this network using a non-linear “staircase” activation function:</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)=\psi_{s}\cdot\lfloor x\rfloor+\psi_{zero}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)=\psi_{s}\cdot\lfloor x\rfloor+\psi_{zero},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\cdot\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\cdot\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> is the rounding operator, the latent code <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\theta^{*}/s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\theta^{*}/s</annotation></semantics></math> and parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\{s,z_{zero}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\{s,z_{zero}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The objective is to minimize the quantization error:</p>
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi_{s},\psi_{zero}}\|\theta^{*}-(\psi_{s}\cdot\lfloor x\rfloor+\psi_{zero})\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi_{s},\psi_{zero}}\|\theta^{*}-(\psi_{s}\cdot\lfloor x\rfloor+\psi_{zero})\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This formulation strictly restricts the output values to the discrete set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{k\cdot\psi_{s}+\psi_{zero}:k\in\mathbb{Z}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k\cdot\psi_{s}+\psi_{zero}:k\in\mathbb{Z}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is to approximate the lattice set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>3 </span>Pruning as Multiplicative Masking Networks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical pruning approximates <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}\approx M\odot\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}\approx M\odot\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the compressor as a Gated Network:</p>
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)=\sigma(\psi_{mask})\odot\theta^{*}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}(x)=\sigma(\psi_{mask})\odot\theta^{*},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{mask}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{mask}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable mask scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The compression task is to find the optimal binary mask structure that minimizes the approximation error under a sparsity constraint <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E23X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\psi_{mask}}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi_{mask}}\|\theta^{*}-(\sigma(\psi_{mask})\odot\theta^{*})\|_{F}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\psi_{mask}}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi_{mask}}\|\theta^{*}-(\sigma(\psi_{mask})\odot\theta^{*})\|_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E23Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad\|\sigma(\psi_{mask})\|_{0}\leq k." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad\|\sigma(\psi_{mask})\|_{0}\leq k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Heaviside step function (or its differentiable surrogate, Gumbel-Softmax), the term <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\psi_{mask})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\psi_{mask})</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the discrete binary mask <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that the output lies strictly on the pruning set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Sigma}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Sigma}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>4 </span>Ergodic Systems as Recurrent Neural Networks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical ergodic compression generates weights via the iteration of a chaotic map: <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}=T(x_{t});w_{t}=P(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}=T(x_{t});w_{t}=P(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the compressor <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> as a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) with the hidden state <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E24X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma(W_{rec}h_{t}+b_{rec})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma(W_{rec}h_{t}+b_{rec})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E24Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=W_{out}h_{t+1}+b_{out}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=W_{out}h_{t+1}+b_{out}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The initial seed of the trajectory is set to a constant <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal is to learn the “laws of physics” (RNN weights) and the “initial condition” (seed) that reproduce the target weight vector:</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi}\sum_{t=1}^{d}\|\theta_{t}^{*}-y_{t}\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>ψ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\psi}\mathcal{L}=\min_{\psi}\sum_{t=1}^{d}\|\theta_{t}^{*}-y_{t}\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By the Universal Approximation Theorem for neural networks, there exists a parameter set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> such that the RNN dynamics approximate the chaotic map <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing the compressor to unroll the exact ergodic trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>5 </span>Knowledge distillation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, the paradigm of KD is based on training smaller neural networks. The proof is completed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29768v1/Overview.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/280;" width="476" height="280" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>An overview of the proposed <span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> framework. It uses a “Compression–Decompression” architecture that encodes discrete weight parameter tensors with lightweight INRs and reconstructs the original weights at inference time.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Big2Small</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our theorem establishes the feasibility that a specific neural network can encapsulate classical model compression methods. This implies the possibility of constructing a small neural network to represent weights of a larger one, termed as <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span>. The <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> framework introduces a novel system-level approach to neural network compression, which is essentially to translate the idea of INRs from data to a network. Therefore, we can also call our method INR network compressor. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C5 Knowledge distillation ‣ III-C Theory II: Structural Equivalence ‣ III A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> departs from pruning and quantization that directly reduce weights. Instead, it uses a “Compression–Decompression” architecture that encodes discrete weight tensors with lightweight INRs. This design substantially reduces storage by saving the encoded representations and reconstructing the original weights at inference time. In doing so, it aims to preserve the network’s salient information, thereby maintaining competitive accuracy relative to traditional compression methods. Consequently, we adopt INRs as our primary compression module, which can be further combined with quantization and other compression methods to enhance compression efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed paradigm offers a new direction for model compression. The flexibility of the INR structure allows for unique advantages, such as the ability to iteratively compress the INR itself by other compression methods to achieve higher compression ratios—a capability not completed in traditional methods. Notably, our method differs from the recently proposed neural-network-based compression approach, Riemannian Neural Dynamics (RieM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, which, to our knowledge, is the first to use one neural network to compress the weights of another and achieve satisfactory results. However, RieM focuses on compressing individual weight parameters and does not provide an end-to-end, neural-network-driven compression pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To be specific, RieM works by regressing each pretrained weight matrix with a separate Riemannian metric and neuron embeddings, using the original weights as reconstruction targets. This multi-stage, hand-engineered pipeline requires extensive hyperparameter tuning (e.g., <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, merging thresholds) and substantial offline optimization time per model (from hours to days). It does not provide a unified compressor–decompressor framework that can be reused across architectures or tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training Big2Small</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the target model be represented by a set of parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=[\mathbf{w}_{1},\mathbf{w}_{2},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{N}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=[\mathbf{w}_{1},\mathbf{w}_{2},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{N}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight tensor of the layer <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. The INR-reconstructed parameters are <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Theta}=[\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{2},\dots,\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{N}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Theta}=[\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{2},\dots,\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{N}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, let us introduce the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Big2Small</span> framework based on Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C5 Knowledge distillation ‣ III-C Theory II: Structural Equivalence ‣ III A unifying Theoretical Framework of Model Compression ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>Outlier-Aware Preprocessing</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The raw weights often contain outliers that are difficult for neural networks to approximate accurately. We therefore introduce a preprocessing strategy prior to INR training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, motivated by findings that roughly 1% of weight outliers dominate quantization error <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, we locate the indices of the smallest and largest <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math> values in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{\min}=\operatorname{argsort}(\mathbf{w})[:k],\quad\mathcal{I}_{\max}=\operatorname{argsort}(\mathbf{w})[-k:]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>argsort</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>argsort</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{\min}=\operatorname{argsort}(\mathbf{w})[:k],\quad\mathcal{I}_{\max}=\operatorname{argsort}(\mathbf{w})[-k:],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1\%\times|\mathbf{w}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">%</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1\%\times|\mathbf{w}|</annotation></semantics></math>. We store these indices and their exact values in a dictionary. During inference, we patch these values back into the reconstructed tensor, thereby bypassing approximation error for extreme entries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we normalize the remaining “body” of the distribution to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to match the active regime of the INR used for compression. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> be the global extrema of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. The normalized weights are defined as</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{norm}}=2\cdot\frac{\mathbf{w}-w_{\min}}{w_{\max}-w_{\min}}-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>norm</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{norm}}=2\cdot\frac{\mathbf{w}-w_{\min}}{w_{\max}-w_{\min}}-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which stabilizes the optimization landscape for the neural compressor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we construct a normalized coordinate grid <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> that encodes spatial positions. For a tensor with spatial dimensions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, the coordinate for index <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i,j}=\left(2\cdot\frac{i}{\max(H-1,1)}-1,\;2\cdot\frac{j}{\max(W-1,1)}-1\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i,j}=\left(2\cdot\frac{i}{\max(H-1,1)}-1,\;2\cdot\frac{j}{\max(W-1,1)}-1\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">providing a resolution-agnostic parameterization that enables the INR to learn a continuous function underlying the discrete weight matrix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>Data-Free Compression Using INRs</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our objective is to learn a compact representation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Phi|\ll|\Theta|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi|\ll|\Theta|</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing reconstruction error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a weight tensor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{out}}\times d_{\text{in}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we view the weights as samples from a continuous signal on a grid. We define a coordinate-to-value mapping (the INR network compressor) <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}[\mathbf{x}]=f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}[\mathbf{x}]=f_{\phi}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each layer, we train the model by solving</p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}=\arg\min_{\phi}\mathcal{L}\big(\mathbf{w},f_{\phi}(\cdot)\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}=\arg\min_{\phi}\mathcal{L}\big(\mathbf{w},f_{\phi}(\cdot)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined and detailed later.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After INR training, we optionally quantize the INR weights to further reduce storage. For a per-tensor uniform <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>-bit quantizer with scale <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and zero-point <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, we use</p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\operatorname{clip}\Big(\operatorname{round}\big(\tfrac{\hat{\mathbf{w}}-z}{s}\big),\;0,\;2^{b}-1\Big),\quad\tilde{\mathbf{w}}=s\,q+z," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>round</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mn> 2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\operatorname{clip}\Big(\operatorname{round}\big(\tfrac{\hat{\mathbf{w}}-z}{s}\big),\;0,\;2^{b}-1\Big),\quad\tilde{\mathbf{w}}=s\,q+z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\tfrac{\max(\hat{\mathbf{w}})-\min(\hat{\mathbf{w}})}{2^{b}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\tfrac{\max(\hat{\mathbf{w}})-\min(\hat{\mathbf{w}})}{2^{b}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\min(\hat{\mathbf{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\min(\hat{\mathbf{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dequantized weights used at inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is noteworthy that we skip batch normalization (BN) layers during compression because their statistics are essential for stable inference. This is actually a common practice in model compression. We compress only convolutional and linear layers larger than 100 KB to limit overhead and control error. Moreover, if a layer’s reconstruction MSE exceeds 0.01, we discard the compressed version and keep the original.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the stored model package includes: i) uncompressed parameters; ii) trained INR weights; iii) the outlier dictionary; and iv) position metadata required for inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Structure of Implicit Neural Representation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our INR models follow the structure in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, employing a dual-MLP encoder for the input coordinates. The overall architecture is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-B The Structure of Implicit Neural Representation ‣ IV Big2Small ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. i) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Synthesis network</span>: Based on SIREN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> with a positional encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, this branch maps input coordinates to reconstructed weight values with strong high-frequency representation capacity. ii) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Modulation network</span>: A learnable modulator conditions and parameterizes periodic activations, capturing generalizable local functional structure in the weights.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29768v1/INR.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>The structure of INR.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further enhance high-frequency fidelity, we incorporate a frequency-aware loss function that penalizes first-order gradient discrepancies and reweights residuals in the Fourier domain, encouraging accurate reconstruction of both low- and high-frequency components.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>1 </span>Frequency-Modulated INR</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input coordinate vector <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the modulator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and the positional encoder <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously map the input via a dual-MLP:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}&amp;\mathbf{h}_{0}=\mathrm{Linear}_{\text{head}}\big(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{x})\big),\\
&amp;\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{Linear}(\mathbf{x}).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Linear</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}&amp;\mathbf{h}_{0}=\mathrm{Linear}_{\text{head}}\big(\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{x})\big),\\
&amp;\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{Linear}(\mathbf{x}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, the modulator output with a base frequency <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a spatially varying frequency vector <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})=\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x})+\omega_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})=\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x})+\omega_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For the weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and bias <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th hidden layer, the output is:</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i+1}=\sin\!\left(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})\odot(\mathbf{W}_{i}\mathbf{h}_{i}+\mathbf{b}_{i})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.243em;" width="1.243em"><mi>sin</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i+1}=\sin\!\left(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{x})\odot(\mathbf{W}_{i}\mathbf{h}_{i}+\mathbf{b}_{i})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise multiplication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The modulator predicts a location-dependent frequency map that dynamically scales the periodicity of sinusoidal activations in the synthesis branch, controlling spectral bias locally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. Unlike SIREN with the fixed <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sin\big(\omega_{0}(\mathbf{W}\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{b})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐖𝐡</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sin\big(\omega_{0}(\mathbf{W}\mathbf{h}+\mathbf{b})\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, the effective frequency is a learned function of the input. The final hidden state is mapped by a linear tail to the target shape:</p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\mathrm{Linear}_{\text{tail}}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Linear</mi><mtext>tail</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\mathrm{Linear}_{\text{tail}}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The positional encoder projects the inputs to a Fourier feature space to efficiently capture higher-frequency structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. For the frequency band <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and the scalar input <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i,j}(x_{j})=\big[\sin(\sigma^{i}\pi x_{j}),\;\cos(\sigma^{i}\pi x_{j})\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i,j}(x_{j})=\big[\sin(\sigma^{i}\pi x_{j}),\;\cos(\sigma^{i}\pi x_{j})\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{0,\dots,L-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{0,\dots,L-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> band length, and scaling factor <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The encoder concatenates the raw input and all sinusoidal expansions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{x})=\big[\mathbf{x},\;\gamma_{0}(\mathbf{x}),\;\gamma_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\;\dots,\;\gamma_{L-1}(\mathbf{x})\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\mathbf{x})=\big[\mathbf{x},\;\gamma_{0}(\mathbf{x}),\;\gamma_{1}(\mathbf{x}),\;\dots,\;\gamma_{L-1}(\mathbf{x})\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent aliasing, we apply a Nyquist bandwidth limiter that chooses the maximum band via</p>
<table id="S4.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\approx\big\lfloor\log_{2}(\text{Nyquist Rate})\big\rfloor=\big\lfloor\log_{2}(S/2)\big\rfloor," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Nyquist Rate</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\approx\big\lfloor\log_{2}(\text{Nyquist Rate})\big\rfloor=\big\lfloor\log_{2}(S/2)\big\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes spatial resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we size the INR by the target compression ratio <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> raw parameters, the base hidden width is controlled by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\max\big(32,\sqrt{P/(4r)}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\max\big(32,\sqrt{P/(4r)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we use six hidden layers arranged as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[h,h,h,h/2,h/2,h/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[h,h,h,h/2,h/2,h/2]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>2 </span>Frequency-Aware Loss Function</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although pre-trained weights appear near-Gaussian distribution, the INR reconstructions may underfit higher frequencies. We therefore adopt a frequency-aware objective to encourage low- and high-frequency recovery. The total loss combines MSE, Gradient Difference (Grad) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, and Focal Frequency (Freq) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> terms:</p>
<table id="S4.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\alpha\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{mse}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{grad}}+\psi\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{freq}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mse</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>grad</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>freq</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\alpha\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{mse}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{grad}}+\psi\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{freq}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}1,\;\beta{=}0.5,\;\psi{=}0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}1,\;\beta{=}0.5,\;\psi{=}0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the Grad loss is primarily used in video prediction to reduce blurring at boundaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. For a 2D weight <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, discrete gradients along horizontal (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>) and vertical (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>) directions are</p>
<table id="S4.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{h}\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}_{i,j+1}-\mathbf{w}_{i,j},\quad\nabla_{v}\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}_{i+1,j}-\mathbf{w}_{i,j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{h}\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}_{i,j+1}-\mathbf{w}_{i,j},\quad\nabla_{v}\mathbf{w}=\mathbf{w}_{i+1,j}-\mathbf{w}_{i,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the gradient loss minimizes first-order differences:</p>
<table id="S4.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{grad}}=\big\|\nabla_{h}\hat{\mathbf{w}}-\nabla_{h}\mathbf{w}\big\|_{2}^{2}+\big\|\nabla_{v}\hat{\mathbf{w}}-\nabla_{v}\mathbf{w}\big\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>grad</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{grad}}=\big\|\nabla_{h}\hat{\mathbf{w}}-\nabla_{h}\mathbf{w}\big\|_{2}^{2}+\big\|\nabla_{v}\hat{\mathbf{w}}-\nabla_{v}\mathbf{w}\big\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the Freq loss was first introduced in the image reconstruction task to address the high-frequency periodic patterns missing issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>. The frequency loss operates in the Fourier domain via a 2D FFT:</p>
<table id="S4.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{freq}}=\big\|\mathrm{FFT}(\hat{\mathbf{w}})-\mathrm{FFT}(\mathbf{w})\big\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>freq</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>FFT</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>FFT</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{freq}}=\big\|\mathrm{FFT}(\hat{\mathbf{w}})-\mathrm{FFT}(\mathbf{w})\big\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Optimizing in frequency space penalizes global high-frequency discrepancies that may appear as small local errors in the spatial domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Configuration</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we compress some mainstream image recognition models, such as UNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, ResNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and Swin-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, and validate their performance on the image classification and segmentation tasks. These pretrained models are collected from the PyTorch Image Models (timm) repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. Several DFMC methods are utilized as the baselines. We use their default hyperparameter settings in the experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For our method, we set the initial learning rate to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> with the AdamW optimizer. The learning rate scheduler is CosineAnnealingLR and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{max}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{max}=200</annotation></semantics></math>. All INRs are executed 10,000 epochs to ensure convergence. To compare with other baselines, we compress the model by a ratio of 1.5 in 32-bit full-precision training and quantize it to lower bits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Compression Results</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Classification on ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite></span>. Experimental results are presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-B Model Compression Results ‣ V Experiments ‣ Big2Small: A Unifying Neural Network Framework for Model Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>. The Big2Small demonstrates competitive performance across different architectures when compared to other compression techniques. For ResNet18, Big2Small-6bit achieves a compression ratio of 5.9 with a model size of only 7.6 MB, outperforming DSG and Squant while maintaining a higher compression rate. In the ResNet50 experiments, Big2Small-8bit notably secures the highest Top-1 accuracy of 73.98% among the compressed models, surpassing UDFC and RieM despite using a slightly lower compression ratio of 7.7. Furthermore, for Swin Transformer, our method also achieves the best accuracy in the same model size level. Compared to conventional quantization methods that only compress models to a fixed size, the Big2Small is more flexible. The original model can first be compressed into the format of 32bit and then quantized to a smaller size.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Comparison of different compression methods for three notable image recognition networks on the ImageNet dataset. W/A means the numbers of weight/activation bits.</figcaption>
